{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\begin{proposition}[Detection Lower Bound for Permutation Granger Test]
\label{prop:detection-bound}
Consider a permutation Granger causality test with $B = 5000$ permutations, BH FDR correction at level $q = 0.05$ across $m = 8$ metric pairs, applied to a first-differenced time series of length $T$.

\noindent\textbf{(i) Minimum detectable effect size.}
The signal strength $\beta$ required for $50\%$ detection power satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \beta_{50}(T) \;=\; 28.50 \;\cdot\; T^{-1.16}, \quad R^2 = 0.996,
  \label{eq:beta50}
\end{equation}
calibrated via Monte Carlo simulation ($n = 100$ replications per $(T, \beta)$ cell).

\noindent\textbf{(ii) Joint signal--response model.}
The PermG forward detection rate is modeled as a T-dependent sigmoid:
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{rate}(\alpha, T) \;=\; \frac{0.70}{1 + \exp\!\bigl(-100.0\,(\alpha - \alpha_c(T))\bigr)}, \quad R^2 = 0.883,
  \label{eq:joint-sigmoid}
\end{equation}
fitted jointly to $T = 10$ and $T = 20$ data, excluding the synchronization dip at $\alpha \in [0.55, 0.65]$ where structural non-monotonicity invalidates the Granger framework (\S\ref{sec:sync-dip}).

\noindent\textbf{(iii) Detection boundary.}
The critical mixing ratio below which PermG cannot reliably detect forward causation ($\geq 50\%$ of pairs) follows:
\begin{equation}
  \alpha_c(T) \;=\; 0.193 + 75.68 \;\cdot\; T^{-2.41}.
  \label{eq:alpha-c}
\end{equation}
Predictions: $\alpha_c(5) = 1.00$, $\alpha_c(10) = 0.49$, $\alpha_c(15) = 0.30$, $\alpha_c(20) = 0.25$, $\alpha_c(25) = 0.23$.
The $T = 20$ predictions are validated against six held-out experiments.
\end{proposition}", "stderr": "", "return_code": 0}, "error": null, "metadata": {"elapsed_seconds": 0.44}}
